From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 94 21:28:37 EST
Subject: [#94-11-398] NANOTECH: Drexler on self-replication 

My interpretation of the Drexler quote from Ed Regis which is given below is
that Drexler has refined his ideas so that now he realizes he does not need
self-contained replicators. Replication is still important.  Only selected
parts of a system are replicated.

Ed Regis quotes Drexler 3/11/92:  

"Another change [from my earlier conception of nanotechnology] at the systems
level has been an increasing appreciation for the fact that virtually all of
the advantages that one might see in self-replicating machines can be
obtained without building a self-replicating machine, if by a
self-replicating machine you mean something that takes simple raw materials
and makes a copy of itself based on its internal information, something like
genes.  The developments that I am currently exploring that are described in
the new book involve mechanisms building other mechanisms like themselves but
in response to a steady stream of instructions from the outside, and that's a
big advantage because it means the mechanism is simpler.  It doesn't have an
on-board computer.  It doesn't have an on-board information storage system
and so it's a much better place to start than attempting to build a genuine
self-replicating machine.  And in the longer term you have large systems, and
whether one builds something that can build a copy of itself at that point is
quite optional."  

END quote from Drexler 3/11/92.

Basically, Drexler is saying (in my opinion) that it is not necessary to
build a completely self-contained self-replicator.  However, replication is
still very important to his approach.  He is saying it is not necessary to
replicate all parts of a system. For example, one need not replicate the
computer control and instruction memory.  Instead, one may replicate just
part of a system. Above he calls this process ``mechanisms building other
mechanisms like themselves''.  The final paragraph of my previous message
was exactly about this issue.

Begin quote from previous message:

There are actually nuances to the meaning of the term replicate.  Some
systems Drexler describes are not really self-contained self-replicators.
For example, Drexler has clever ideas for communicating with systems using
modulated pressure via mechanical transducers (pg 472). These systems do not
need to directly incorporate computers and stored instructions. Instead, the
computers and instructions remain at the macroscale level. Therefore the
systems are not self-contained self-replicators because the computers and
instructions are not replicated.  

End quote

As indicated on page 472 section 16.3.1 of Nanosystems ``onboard computing,
information storage, and power supply systems can be replaced by transducers
that respond to externally imposed acoustic signals.'' The key insight is
that the transducers can be copied instead of copying the computing,
information storage, and power supply systems. The quote I gave in 
the last message from Section 16.5.4 page 487 used the term ``replication''
to refer to this type of non self-contained replication.

Ed Regis says:

I myself don't see anything particularly challenging about self-replication,
since if a molecular manufactuing unit can build systems of any arbitrary
complexity, then building another system exactly like itself is just a
special case of whatever else it can build.  

End quote

I think we agree here. Self-replication is not a fundamental barrier in my
opinion. It is just easier to start bootstraping with a simplified system.
                      gfs

