From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 1994 19:15:48 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-399] Industrial Revolution Economics

Gregory Sullivan:
> Nick Szabo (and others) think it will take more than one hundred
> years to develop nanotech.  I think the key developments in nanotech will
> occur long before one hundred years.  

Of course, it depends what we mean by "nanotech".  If we are talking 
about manufacturing processes with nanometer precision, we already have 
those in some narrow domains, and those domains will widen as the
century progresses, to more materials, more degrees of freedom,
more features per second, and larger markets.  Here are _specific_ 
technologies that probably won't appear within the next one hundred 
years:

* Self-reproducing diamondoid machines with sufficient closure to 
bring most of the postulated the benefits of exponential growth.
* Utility fog.
* Cell repair machines.
* Cryonics reanimation.
* AI or uploading (although computers and robots will be much faster 
	and more sophisticated).

I do expect many of these to occur within the next two hundred years,
ie most of them will emerge some time in the 22nd century.  The
remaining will likely appear by the end of the 23rd century.

Note that this is in no way to denigrate the work on these technologies,
which is of profound importance.  That importance, however, consists
of the consequences of the far future on the life choices we make today,
not in terms of business investment decisions, or other short-term
ways we usually evualate technology.  It is no surprise that out of
the $billions of worldwide venture capital, there is no significant
investment in Drexlerian nanotechnology; the vast majority of businessmen 
and scientists agree with my timeline, and correctly dismiss nanotech out 
of hand as an investment possibility.

BTW, Bill Gates is a great fan of Drexler.  When I talked to him in
1987, he was waving around a copy of _Engines of Creation_, quoting
from it, and declaring that he shared that vision of the future.  So 
he's hardly unaware of Drexlerian nanotech, but it's clear that it's 
not within his investment timeframe.

Here are technologies I do expect to occur in the 21st century:

* Orders of magnitude faster computers (although I do expect
Moore's law will break down).
* Atomic (probe needle) storage, terabytes on a coin.
* Reversible computation.
* Extinct species reanimation.
* 3D microlithography, ubiquitous micro robots, and atomic-scale
precision manufacturing as an adjunct to lithography and assembly.
* Smart drugs (exact features and consequences highly unpredictable)
* Neural extensions (brain growth, chip implants, etc.)
* Smart contracts and privacy technologies widely deployed, in arms 
race with surveillence technology.  "Accountability sans extortion".
* Multinational small business ubiquitious (a social development
based on travel, communications, and software technologies).
* Green goo.  (If the impact of this is substantial, human population
	could be reduced to below 1 billion people, and the technologies
	in the previous list could be delayed by 100 years or more)
* Space development, primarily automated (not space colonization).

Substantial life extension may or may not occur; that particular technology 
is almost completely unpredictable, since there are no lab or theoretical
breakthroughs that strongly indicate life extension beyond the 100-120 
range.

I may yet have the enjoyment of moving some technologies from the 22nd 
century list to the 21st century.  I would be happy to be proven wrong 
by events, but it would be silly to count on it.

