From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 1994 19:40:53 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-400] NANOTECH: Drexler on self-replication

Ed Regis:
> I myself don't see anything particularly challenging about self-replication,
> since if a molecular manufactuing unit can build systems of any arbitrary
> complexity, then building another system exactly like itself is just a
> special case of whatever else it can build.  

Arbitrary complexity does not mean arbitrary form.  The class of
machines manufactured, while as or more complex than the originals,
might well exclude machines of the specific design, materials, and/or
size of the machines doing such manufacturing.  For example a 6-dof robot, 
given enough time, can be programmed with motions of arbitrary 
complexity, and might put together assemblies of much greater complexity
than itself, but that hardly means it could manufacture a copy of itself
from raw materials.

There is no such thing as a manufacturing unit that can build systems
of arbitrary form.  The diamondoid machines Drexler has partially
designed certainly can't do that.  There are limited to a specific set 
of starter molecules, crystalline structures, surface properties, chemical
properties, environmental constrains, ranges of motion, etc.

Exponential growth is limited by the degree of closure.  For example
if the system is only 99.99% closed, requiring 1/100 of 1% "vitamins",
then growth is only linear with the amount of vitamins, by a factor
of 1,000.  To achieve more growth we need to achieve greater closure.
Thus the devil is in the details.  For example, do the flourocarbon 
molecular bearnings have the proper electrostatic properties, and are they 
environmentally benign in large quantities?  What is the specific
design for the molecular extractors and reactors used to move molecules
from the environment, onto the conveyor belt, onto the manipulator
arm tip, and then onto the work surface?

Only a very few simple cases of this highly complex process have been
worked out, eg the Musgrave mechanosynthesis of diamond with carbene
molecules.  (And then only the final reaction, not the manipulator that 
causes the reaction, or a method of getting the carbene onto the tip
in the first place).  It is doubtful that diamondoid systems can 
achieve anything close to self-replication, whether supplied by information 
from the outside or not.  Such a capacity has most certainly not
been demonstrated.  This is not to say that diamondoid nanobots won't
make dandy agile manufacturing platforms, and might not come closer
to closure than any other manufacturing system, but that they would 
still fall far short of the suggested capacity for exponential growth.

Nick Szabo					szabo@netcom.com

